00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:05.430 align:start position:0%
amira manchiaro was arrested in the  ethiopian capital addis ababa accused of  promoting terrorism he was working as a  freelance journalist for the associated  press  the us-based news agency has called the  allegations baseless two other  journalists were also arrested they face  up to 15 years in prison if convicted of  violating the emergency laws brought in  last month  the annual global roundup by reporters  without borders paints a bleak picture  of media safety and freedom particularly  in ethiopia  the correspondent of the new york times  has been kicked out of the country which  was unprecedented since abby took office  in in  2018 there is this communication  blackout in the northern part of the  country and now we are just not able to  do independent journalism anymore  because only the official narrative  about the war is allowed for journalists  it's been a tough year for journalists  around the world reporters without  borders says at least 46 journalists  have been killed this year because of  the stories they were reporting and  nearly 500 are being detained  65 are being held hostage the most  dangerous countries for media workers  are mexico and afghanistan followed by  yemen and india  one of the reason why there are so many  journalists detained this year up to 20  percent is mainly because the  deterioration of the situation in three  countries including myanmar and china  and the belarus  reporters without borders says its  findings are alarming but remarkably the  number of journalists killed this year  is the lowest since it began counting 25  years ago but the number of journalists  being detained is the highest and in  their absence it says vital voices  remain silent  victoria gatenby al jazeera
 

